<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cfa-franc">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">CFA franc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as West African CFA franc, Central African CFA franc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The CFA franc is the name of two currencies used in West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in December 1945 for its African colonies. After independence it survived as the West African CFA franc and the Central African CFA franc, each issued by a regional central bank, pegged first to the French franc and since 1999 to the euro and guaranteed by the French Treasury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cfa-franc.jpg" alt="CFA franc" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jarry1250 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CFA_Franc_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The CFA franc was created on 26 December 1945, together with the CFP franc, because the French franc was weak after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ratified the Bretton Woods Agreement in December 1945, the French franc was devalued to set a fixed rate against the US dollar. New currencies were created in the colonies to spare them the strong devaluation, which made it easier for them to import goods from France and harder to export to France. French officials presented the decision as generosity. Between 1945 and 1958 CFA stood for Colonies françaises d'Afrique, and from 1958 until independence for Communauté française d'Afrique.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name covers two currencies that have the same value but are not interchangeable. The West African CFA franc is issued by the Central Bank of the West African States at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> for eight countries: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. The Central African CFA franc is issued by the Bank of the Central African States at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaounde">Yaoundé</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. Both are guaranteed by the French Treasury.</p><p>The rate against the French franc was changed only twice. From 1945 one CFA franc was worth 1.70 French francs; from 17 October 1948 it was worth 2. On 12 January 1994 an overnight devaluation of 50 per cent halved its value. When the euro replaced the French franc on 1 January 1999 the rate became 655.957 CFA francs to the euro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The zone has changed since independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> left in 1960 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in 1962, rejoining in 1984; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> left in 1973, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, a former Portuguese colony, joined in 1997. Critics hold that the currency restricts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the member states and makes national monetary policy all but impossible, and some object that the notes are printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Defenders argue that it stabilises the national currencies and eases trade with France.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “CFA franc” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>CFA franc</b>, created in 1945 for the French colonies of Africa, survives in reformed form as the West African CFA franc, linking several of the successor states to France through monetary cooperation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
